# OOP in Java - Lecture 3: Objects Deep Dive

These are topic-aligned study notes for ["Objects Deep Dive | Size, Call by Value vs Reference | Shallow vs Deep Copy | Java Full Course #14"](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7yd5qO2TPr0), not a verbatim transcript.

## Objects and memory

- A class describes a type; an object is an instance created at runtime.
- An object contains its instance fields and JVM-managed object metadata. A reference is a value used to locate an object; it is not the object itself.
- The JVM specification does not define a fixed byte size for an object. Actual size depends on the JVM, object headers, field layout, alignment, and options such as compressed references.
- Use a tool such as Java Object Layout (JOL) when you need to inspect object layout on a particular JVM. A hand calculation based only on field types is not reliable.
- The stack/heap picture is a useful teaching model, not a complete description of JVM execution. JIT optimizations can change where values are physically stored.

## Java passes arguments by value

- Java always passes a copy of the argument value to a method.
- For a primitive, the copied value is the primitive itself. Assigning to the parameter does not change the caller's variable.
- For an object, the copied value is the reference. The parameter and caller can refer to the same object, so a method can mutate that object's state.
- Reassigning the parameter only changes its local copy of the reference; it does not redirect the caller's variable.
- This is why Java is not "pass by reference," even though changes made through a shared object reference can be visible to the caller.

## Shallow and deep copies

- A shallow copy creates a new outer object but copies references to nested objects. The two outer objects can therefore share mutable state.
- A deep copy also copies the mutable objects reachable through the outer object, so later mutations are independent (to the depth the copy operation defines).
- Immutable values such as `String` are generally safe to share. For mutable object graphs, define the intended copy behavior explicitly; cycles and shared sub-objects need care.
- In the demo, the shallow copy shares an `Address`; the deep copy creates a separate `Address`.

Run `Lecture3ObjectsDeepDive.java` to see argument passing and copy behavior.

//CODES OF NOTES

public class Lecture3ObjectsDeepDive {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student original = new Student("Mina", new Address("Delhi"));
        Student shallowCopy = new Student(original);
        Student deepCopy = original.deepCopy();

        renameStudent(original);
        System.out.println("Name after method mutation: " + original.getName());

        replaceStudent(original);
        System.out.println("Name after parameter reassignment: " + original.getName());

        original.getAddress().setCity("Mumbai");
        System.out.println("Original city: " + original.getAddress().getCity());
        System.out.println("Shallow copy city: " + shallowCopy.getAddress().getCity());
        System.out.println("Deep copy city: " + deepCopy.getAddress().getCity());
        System.out.println("Shallow copy shares address: "
                + (original.getAddress() == shallowCopy.getAddress()));
        System.out.println("Deep copy shares address: "
                + (original.getAddress() == deepCopy.getAddress()));
    }

    private static void renameStudent(Student student) {
        student.setName("Asha");
    }

    private static void replaceStudent(Student student) {
        student = new Student("Ravi", new Address("Pune"));
    }
}

final class Student {
    private String name;
    private final Address address;

    Student(String name, Address address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.address = address;
    }

    Student(Student other) {
        this(other.name, other.address);
    }

    String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    Address getAddress() {
        return address;
    }

    Student deepCopy() {
        return new Student(name, new Address(address.getCity()));
    }
}

final class Address {
    private String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }

    String getCity() {
        return city;
    }

    void setCity(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }
}
